// ================= 4. Conclusion =================
C.push(h1("4. Conclusion"));
C.push(p("An adaptive TVD reconstruction whose limiter value is selected per face by the boundary-variation-diminishing principle has been extended to unstructured grids and embedded, as the linear candidate, in a three-member BVD scheme with two THINC/QQ sigmoid candidates and a MOOD safeguard. Two findings are emphasized. First, the direct unstructured transplantation of the structured-grid construction is unconditionally unstable: the per-face zero-jump selection severs the coherence of the cell-wise reconstruction profile, allowing nascent extrema to be amplified through all faces simultaneously, and the one-dimensional total-variation argument does not survive the replacement of exact directional slope ratios by projected least-squares ratios. Face-value boundedness alone is demonstrably insufficient. Second, fusing the adaptive limiter with a vertex-monotone MLP-type bound-clamping each face-side increment to the reach of the cell's vertex-limited linear profile-restores the local-extremum-diminishing mechanism, yields a robust scheme in all experiments, and does so without sacrificing the zero-dissipation faces where the data are smooth, because the clamp is inactive exactly there. The fused candidate spans diffusive to compressive limiter values in a single pass and thereby simplifies the BVD candidate set to one linear and two sigmoid members. In the benchmark suite the proposed scheme maintains slip-line and vortical structures at a level comparable to the established MUSCL-THINC/QQ-BVD baseline while providing strictly bounded scalar transport and a consistently larger positivity margin, at a linear-stage cost below that of the baseline family. The construction is dimension-agnostic and carries over to mixed-element grids without modification of any formula; among the questions left open we note the extension of the vertex-bound machinery to strongly anisotropic elements, a characteristic-wise variant for flows where primitive-variable selection is insufficient, the interaction with implicit time integration, and a sharper theoretical statement of the stability of the fused construction beyond the LED mechanism identified here."));

// ================= Appendix A =================
C.push(h1("Appendix A. Interval-intersection logic of the adaptive candidate"));
C.push(p("For Δq = q̄ⱼ − q̄ᵢ and the projected ratios (11), define the four extreme face values"));
C.push(eq("q_mm^L = q̄ᵢ + ½Φ_mm(r^L)Δq,  q_sb^L = q̄ᵢ + ½Φ_sb(r^L)Δq,  q_mm^R = q̄ⱼ − ½Φ_mm(r^R)Δq,  q_sb^R = q̄ⱼ − ½Φ_sb(r^R)Δq.", "A.1"));
C.push(p("The admissible interval of the left side is [q_mm^L, q_sb^L] (orientation given by the sign of Δq) and of the right side [q_sb^R, q_mm^R]. When the reachability indicator (12) satisfies A ≥ 2, the two intervals intersect or one contains the other; which configuration holds is decided by the signs of"));
C.push(eq("B = (q_sb^L − q_mm^R)(q_sb^L − q_sb^R),   C = (q_sb^R − q_mm^L)(q_sb^R − q_sb^L).", "A.2"));
C.push(p("The shared segment ξ is: (b) B ≤ 0 and C ≤ 0: ξ = [q_sb^L, q_sb^R] (genuine crossing of the superbee values); (c) B ≤ 0 and C ≥ 0: ξ = [q_mm^L, q_sb^L] (left interval contained in right); (d) B ≥ 0 and C ≤ 0: ξ = [q_mm^R, q_sb^R] (right contained in left); (e) B ≥ 0 and C ≥ 0: the intervals have crossed without sharing a segment, and the arithmetic mean of the two cell averages is used as the shared state. In cases (b)-(d), elementary case-checking shows ξ to be contained in the admissible intervals of both sides, so any q* ∈ ξ is TVD-admissible for either side with respect to the projected ratios; in particular the endpoint selected by the upwind rule-ξ_max if a_f (q̄ᵢ − q̄ⱼ) < 0 and ξ_min otherwise, with a_f the face-normal advection speed-is admissible. The vertex-monotone clamp (14) is applied after the selection, to both face sides independently. Boundary faces of the domain, which possess no neighbour state, use the vertex-limited gradient projection q̄ᵢ + clamp(∇qᵢ·(x_f−xᵢ)) directly, with the clamp bound taken from the face-neighbour extrema of the boundary cell."));

// ================= Appendix B =================
C.push(h1("Appendix B. Properties of the fused limiter bound"));
C.push(p("We record the elementary properties of the clamp (13)-(14) used in Section 2.6. (P1) Boundedness: since ψᵢ^LMP ≤ 2 and the target t lies between the adjacent averages, the clamped face value lies between q̄ᵢ and q̄ᵢ + δᵢ,f, both of which respect the vertex-neighbourhood extrema by the definition of ψᵢ^LMP; hence no face value can exceed the local data range. (P2) Extremum pinning: if q̄ᵢ is a strict extremum of its vertex neighbourhood, then room_v < 0 for every increasing direction and (4) yields ψᵢ^LMP = 0, so δᵢ,f = 0 for all faces of i and every face value of i equals q̄ᵢ; the cell contributes first-order upwind fluxes only, which are LED. (P3) Smooth-region transparency: if the data are locally monotone and well-resolved, the vertex rooms are O(h) while the profile increments are O(h), and for the exact linear profile the ratio in (4) tends to a value ≥ 1; the clamp interval then contains the zero-variation target and (14) is the identity, so the adaptive candidate's zero-dissipation faces are unaffected. (P4) Single-profile coherence: all faces of cell i are clamped against the same pair (ψᵢ^LMP, ∇qᵢ); consequently the vector of face increments of cell i lies in the one-dimensional cone generated by the profile increments, which excludes the all-faces-inward amplification pattern of Section 2.5. These properties fall short of a full multi-dimensional stability proof-none is claimed-but they restore precisely the mechanisms whose absence was identified in the failure analysis, and the numerical evidence of Section 3 supports the resulting construction."));

